SOUTH BAY HEAlTH FOOD STORES DEFEND "ENDANGERED" SUPPLEMENTS by Barbara
Keeler

  "What do dietary supplements and dinosaurs have in common? Nothing
yet. Tell Congress to keep it that way." That's the message on a poster
in Mrs. Gooch, posted next to a telephone on which customers are urged
to call their Congressional representatives and President Clinton.
  Flyers at the table, located next to the sample booth, explain
restrictions the 1990 Nutrition Labeling and Education Act (NLEA),
imposes on dietary supplements. Underscoring the point are mourning
ribbons and endangered stickers beneath the products targeted by FDA
proposed rules.
  Other South Bay stores, including Rainbow Acres in Manhattan Beach,
Lindberg's, the Vitamin Store, and Great Earth, have also set up action
tables.
  In Mrs. Gooch's a flyer announces a march to be held at 10 A.M.
September 10 at the FDA building at Pico and Union, followed by a rally
at 11:30.
  "A lot of our customers are really upset at the prospect of losing
access to dietary supplements," said Annette Peterson, of Mrs. Gooch's
nutrition department.
  "During the last comment period, the FDA received 35,000 letters about
this issue--the biggest response history to an FDA request for comment,"
said Lecia McMullen, nutrition director for Mrs. Gooch's.
  FDA representatives claim the supplement industry is unregulated and
new regulations are needed to protect the public. But Mrs. Gooch's
attorney Loren Israelsen emphasized that existing laws already provide
adequate protection.
  Raising further concern are withdrawals of tryptophan and various
herbs from the market, raids on medical clinics and nutrient dealers,
and arrests of nutrient suppliers and health practitioners.
Congressman Henry Waxman, sponsor of the NLEA, characterized those
actions as abuse of FDA authority.
  "The unapproved food additives clause was never intended to give the
FDA the authority to pull products off the shelves," said Waxman, who
has vowed to oppose additional restrictions on vitamins, supplements,
and herbs. However, NLEA gives the FDA power to further restrict access,
according to critics, including attorney Suzanne Harris. The act
delegates significant regulatory authority to the Secretary of Health
and Humans Services, and its enforcement arm, the FDA.   Open to
wide-ranging interpretation by the FDA, it allows a non-elected body, in
practical terms, to write law. "At first, the NLEA had widespread
support," said former Hermosa Beach resident Jon Rappoport, whose Health
News lectures are now broadcast weekly on KPFK.
  Appearing on Network 23, he explained, "People thought it was just a
law saying 'On the side of the can of beans, we want to know everything
that's in it. Let the consumer be educated.' But once it went to
regulatory phase, the FDA said, `Here's our chance. We'll twist the
meaning of the law, set up a list of regulations that allow us to raid
these stores, and take product off shelves because they're making claims
we don't approve of, or the potency of the vitamin is too high'."
  Supporting the allegations of Rappoport, Harris, and other NLEA
opponents is the May 1992 Final Report of an FDA Dietary Supplement Task
Force (DSTFR). More specific than NLEA, it makes recommendations for
implementation of the law, that would, in effect, limit access to
commonly used supplements.
  According to the executive summary it does so to "...ensure the safety
of dietary supplements...." But McMullen points to another objective at
the end of that paragraph "...and what steps are necessary to ensure
that the existence of dietary supplements on the market does not act as
a disincentive for drug development."
  "How does this goal insure public safety?"  she asks.
 FDA proposed rules for implementation, issued June 18, 1993 adhere to
DSTFR guidelines. For example, they propose to set maximum dosages for
vitamins. They propose to exempt a number of supplements from the
protection of the Proxmire Amendment, passed by Congress in 1976 to
prohibit the FDA from setting maximum potencies of vitamin and mineral
supplements or declaring them drugs.
  Of further concern to consumers is the recommendation that dietary
supplements consumed for therapeutic purposes be regulated as drugs. 
For example, they proposes to regulate amino acids as drugs, which would
increase their cost, and restrict access to those classified as
prescription drugs.
  To sell a product classified as a drug, says Harris, a company must
pay $100,000 application fee and conduct a series of tests, including
animal and human trials. Low end cost estimates run in the millions.]
The requirements that could classify many herbs as drugs and remove
others from the market constitute a cultural bias, according to Gooch's
customer Jacqueline Castellanos, who observes, "For centuries,
Hispanics, Native Americans, and Asians have depended on herbs for
traditional healing and medicine."
  McMullen points out another bias: the inequality of enforcement, and
unequal standards of enforcement, for over-the-counter preparations sold
by pharmaceutical companies and those sold by the health food industry.
  "People died from contaminants in both Tylenol and tryptophan, but
when they found the contaminants, they put Tylenol back on the market,
but not tryptophan."
  Harris agreed.
  "The side effects in the over-the-counter PDR do not raise the same
concern on the part of the FDA as do much more minor effects of single
nutrients, with less statistical significance."
  Explaining the different purposes of drugs and dietary supplements,
Harris said, "People today are interested in health maintenance, or
preventive measures. They don't want to wait until they are really
sick."
  Dr. Hans Kugler of Redondo Beach, agrees.
  "At the Torrance Health Integration Center," he said, "I work with
people who want to stay healthy, live longer, and lower disease risks. I
emphasize the importance of various supplements."
  "In fifteen other industrialized countries, citizens live longer and
are classified as healthier by World Health Organization standards.
Healthy care per capita is half the cost. One factor these countries
have in common is more widespread practice of alternative medicine.
Forty percent of substances doctors prescribe are over-the-counter
vitamins, amino acids, herbs, and botanical."
  Kugler, author of Tripping the Clock, holds a PHD in biochemistry and
is President of the National Health Federation and International Academy
of Alternative Health and Medicine.
  South Bay health food stores, and many citizen groups such as Citizens
for Health, urge support of bills introduced by Senator Orrin Hatch (S.
784), and Congressman Bill Richardson, (R. 1709). These bills would
create a separate category for dietary supplements and allow health
claims based on scientific studies.
  However, some advocates for access to dietary supplements express
reservations about S. 784, and R. 1709.
  Speaking for Freedom for Drug Free Alternatives (FDFA), Harris points
out that because the Hatch bill allows the FDA to declare a dietary
supplement an adulterated food, it opens the door for seizure and
criminal charges.
  Moreover, it gives the FDA authority to judge safety after drug
companies have born the responsibility for the research.
  "I'm usually an optimist," says Hermosa Beach resident and Omnitrition
dealer Mark Whaley, who stopped smoking with the aid of supplements, "I
exhibit at the Whole Life Expo, which, with its array of health aids and
supplements, epitomizes health freedom. If the FDA implements its rules
in December, the Expo at the Airport Hilton on October 1 may be my last
chance to view a complete smorgasborg of health alternatives."
  Whaley and other South Bay Omnitrition dealers sell supplements based
on formulas by former Manhattan Beach authors Dirk Person and Sandy
Shaw, whose book, Life Extension, sparked nationwide interest in
nutrients for optimum health.

