From: freeman@maspar.com (Jay R. Freeman)
Subject: PHIL: Recovering Objectivist

I read _The_Fountainhead_ and _We_the_Living_ when I was in my early to
mid 20s, in graduate school at U. C. Berkeley.  I remember that once when
I was reading one of them on a bus, another passenger asked me what I
thought of the author in terms and tones which with hindsight indicated
he was seriously committed to the philosophy therein.  I replied to the
effect that "she writes an interesting story" -- thinking of _We_the_Living_
-- "but has low intellectual content and no ideas I find appealing".
(I no longer remember why I held that opinion -- this was two decades
ago -- but I do remember that I did hold it.)

My fellow passenger did a double-take and eventually laughed.  Evidently
the more common opinion among persons fond of Rand is rather the opposite
on all points I mentioned.  I have often wondered why Objectivism didn't
"take" with me, since it certainly "took" with lots of my friends and
peers with similar backgrounds, interests and attitudes (which is why
I decided to read some of her books in the first place).  But it
certainly didn't.

I also tried _Atlas_Shrugged_ at that time, but if memory serves, I lost
interest only a little way in.

Tim May mentioned the notions of "we are not our brother's keeper",
self-esteem, self-interest, voluntary transactions, and the efficiency
of the free market as part of Rand's heritage for him.  I think my
own position is fairly close, and already was at the time I did my
reading (which is perhaps why little seemed new).  The difference is
that while I wouldn't use the term "brother's keeper" -- it's too strong
and a little loaded -- nevertheless my personal (and canonically
non-absolute) morality includes a notion of rights which I accord to
others and defend.  This difference can clearly lead to great differences
of opinion at the operational level -- witness the debate in which the
First Extropian Squirrel participated -- but nevertheless I think I speak
much the same language and have many of the same philosophical notions
that most others here do.  I think that many of these notions are much
more widespread than the contention and sense of isolation that I seem
to see here would suggest.

