Date: Thu, 23 Sep 93 02:35:59 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: Meaninglessness (was Miscellaneous)

Mike Price says:

> Pragmatism and positivism are very similar, with their mutual distrust
> of non-operational concepts (which I share).  Also very similar to

It seems to me that the positivists are much nearer to the operationalist
position than are the pragmatists.  The positivists rejected theological
and metaphysical speculation in favour of using the scientific method to
discover "positive truth".  But the pragmatists were not so much
concerned with objectivity as with the practical effects resulting from
the holding of each belief.  No-operational distinction between
hypotheses in the eyes of a positivist would mean that experiments could
not distinguish them; for pragmatists it would be that the hypotheses
were equal in terms of profitability to our lives.
 
When dealing with issues as important as how we might expect to live
forever, it's essential to use the scientific method.  But a pragmatic
approach could be more appropriate at other times.  E.g. assume there's
no operational test for the existence of 'free-will'.  But it might be
that folk who believe that they 'have free-will' would behave more
virtuously (they can't use the excuse of events being outside of their
control).  Or they might decide that such a belief beats the competition
in terms of philosophic satisfaction.  Non-believers could feel that
life is pointless.  As another example, it might be that _some_ people's
behaviour would have been more conducive to furthering most humans' goals
if they had not been moral relativists.  (I'd better point out that I
don't think this would apply to Extropians, but to 'normal' types and
politicians.)

I favour methodological pluralism, but I would agree that the scientific
method is essential for many applications. 

