Date: Wed, 22 Sep 93 19:14:45 PDT
From: Eli Brandt <ebrandt@jarthur.Claremont.EDU>
Subject: Being w/Smart People

> From: eli@suneast.east.sun.com (Elias Israel - SunSelect Engineering)
> If anything, it's being around people with fewer mental skills than I
> that I find more uncomfortable. I get a sickening feeling in the pit of
> my stomach, not unlike the feeling of watching a grisly auto wreck.

I had an experience of this sort, a rather extended experience,
during the summer.  To become certified as a scuba diver, you have
to take a course which consists of a number of training sessions,
probably in somebody's pool, some classroom time, and a few dives.

The class time was quite disturbing.  Not so much the content -- the
instructor was a pretty sharp guy, and knew what he was teaching.
But the other students!  I don't expect everybody in the world
to be familiar with gas physics, but after several hours discussing
the basics of pressure, volume, and so forth, there were people
who were terminally confused about such things as how many
atmospheres of pressure one finds at sea level, whether people
compress to half their volume at 33 feet under water, and how to add
fractions.  It was frightening.  I started wondering about how
society manages to function.

After seeing how little competence is required to become certified,
I'm surprised divers don't float up on the beaches in droves.
Furthermore, the "Advanced Open Water" course consisted of about an
hour of advice on flashlights, plus a weekend of diving.

On the bright side, I was encouraged by a recent exchange on
rec.scuba: one person posted that it was high time for the
government to step in and administer the sport properly, and
promptly received half a dozen responses to the effect of, "What
makes you think the government can do anything right?"  Ain't the
net grand?

